            <div xml:id="d1016">

                <pb facs="0917" n="909" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg909"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163/1020: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-11-28T17:00:00+08:00">November 28, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 28—4:56 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>493. Peiping’s 492, November 28, 4 p.m.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d1016-f1"/>
                    newspaper attack on mission schools. The Embassy believes the editorial quoted
                    in the telegram under reference represents the ideas of the Japanese military
                    who in Japan, Korea and Manchuria have undermined the integrity of
                    mission-operated schools not only because such schools were under <pb
                        facs="0918" n="910" xml:id="pg910"/> foreign influence, but also because
                    they formed centers of liberal and democratic thought. Such concepts are
                    naturally anathema to the Japanese ultra nationalists who seem determined to
                    extirpate all opposition to their ideas of the state and of society.</p>
                <p>It is possible that the editorial coming at this particular time of international
                    strain is the opening gun in a campaign to attempt the eradication or at least
                    the lessening of foreign influence in the educational system of North China.</p>
                <p>Sent to Department, repeated to Chungking, Tientsin. By air mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
